msgid "How extensions are designed"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Why a package has two levels, how it enters the graph without inventing "
"node types, why every write passes through one contract, and why "
"signatures and reviews.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Extended Matrix is one language shared by many communities. "
"Extensions exist so that each community can record what is its own "
"**without the language breaking into dialects**: a study written with a "
"package must stay a study of the Extended Matrix, readable by whoever "
"does not have the package. Every choice described here follows from that."
msgstr ""

msgid "Two levels: data and code"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A package carries **data**, **code**, or both, and the line between them "
"is the line of trust."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Data** are declarations: vocabularies, properties with their units, "
"kinds of reading with their geometry, sets of points, combiners over a "
"closed set of operations, recording sheets, colour schemes. They "
"describe; they do not run. A tool can read them completely, check them "
"completely, and refuse what does not hold. This is why a package of data "
"installs without any question: the worst it can do is describe something "
"badly, and a person can read every line of it. It is also why the same "
"package serves EMStudio, EM Tools in Blender and a StratiGraph node: each"
" tool reads the same declarations."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Code** does what declarations cannot: read a new format, compute "
"outside the closed set, check a study, show a value where the person "
"works. Code runs with the powers of whoever runs it, so it comes with a "
"question of trust, asked once and asked again only when the code asks for"
" more."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The two levels travel in one archive and stay apart: a mixed package "
"keeps its data and its code in separate folders, the data never contain "
"an executable file, and a person may always take the data alone. A "
"community can therefore begin with data only — most needs are met there —"
" and add code later, without changing what its studies already declare."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The closed sets are part of the same choice. A combiner names an "
"operation the core knows (a distance, an angle, a ratio, a mean…), never "
"a formula: a formula in the data would be code in disguise, and would ask"
" for the trust that data are meant to spare."
msgstr ""

msgid "Entering the graph without new node types"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A package adds **kinds**, not types. A point placed on a face is a core "
"``extractor`` node that names its kind in ``data.em_kind`` "
"(``vlt:punto_del_volto``); the width computed from two points is a core "
"property, with its chain to a core ``combiner``; a document of a "
"community is a core ``document`` with its kind. The stratigraphic unit "
"stays the unit of the Extended Matrix, with its relations and its "
"propagation, for everybody."
msgstr ""

msgid "This keeps three promises:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**a study stays one language**: the Matrix, the chronology, the "
"propagation and the export to RDF are computed from core types, the same "
"in every study;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**a study travels**: a tool that does not have the package still draws "
"every node, keeps the labels written with the values, and writes back "
"what it read. A missing package is never a reason to refuse a file;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**a package can change** with its own version, curators and pace, without"
" touching the version of the core or of the file format."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Node and edge types of a package existed in the first draft of the "
"specification and are **suspended in EM 1.6**: a type that changes what "
"the core derives is where the language would split. A need of that kind "
"becomes a proposal for the core, discussed for everybody."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The prefix is the other half of the same idea. Every term a package adds "
"is written ``<prefix>:<name>``, so it never collides with the core or "
"with another package, and a reader always knows whose term it is."
msgstr ""

msgid "The contract of writes"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A plugin, a script in the console and EM Tools change a study through "
"**one door**: ``em.write``. The block becomes a set of changes that "
"passes the contract of the connectors — the command is declared, its "
"slots are filled, an author is present, the capability to write was "
"declared, the edges are those the language allows — and is applied by the"
" program as **one step**, with its line in the Log and an act that "
"records the person and the plugin."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"There is no second channel. The views of the graph a plugin reads are "
"read only, a command declared to read cannot write, and a write that the "
"contract refuses leaves the study as it was. So a value written by a "
"plugin can always be told apart from one written by a person, undone like"
" any other, and read back in the record of the study, with the plugin's "
"name and version."
msgstr ""

msgid "Signatures and reviews"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A package of data asks for no trust; code does, and on a personal "
"computer no program can shut Python in a cage. Trust is therefore built "
"from what can be checked, and the rest is left, clearly, to people."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**A fingerprint** in the index ties every archive to the bytes that were "
"published: an archive changed on the way no longer matches and is "
"refused."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**A signature** says who published those bytes: the holder of a key the "
"person chose to trust, a laboratory or the Extended Matrix. It speaks of "
"origin and integrity, not of quality."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**A review** says that a person read the code: the editor, or the "
"curators of a package that needs it, for that exact code. Its worth is "
"the worth of that reading, and each person decides whose reviews to "
"accept."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**The question of trust** remains for everything else, and the program "
"asks it only when there is something new to ask."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"On a StratiGraph node, where code runs on a shared server, the answer is "
"different: only the administrator installs plugins, and each runs in a "
"closed space with no network, none of the node's files and a time limit. "
"The program proves that the closed space holds before turning any plugin "
"on."
msgstr ""

msgid "See also"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`/explanation/extensions-and-trust`, the same questions from the "
"side of whoever installs."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`SPEC.md <https://github.com/ExtendedMatrix/em-"
"profiles/blob/main/SPEC.md>`__ of em-profiles, the contract every tool "
"keeps."
msgstr ""

